          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is described as first-line treatment for newly diagnosed high blood pressure?",
          "answer": "The guidance describes four drug classes as first-line for most adults without another reason to choose a different drug:\n\n- thiazide-type diuretics\n- angiotensin-converting-enzyme (ACE) inhibitors\n- angiotensin receptor blockers (ARBs)\n- dihydropyridine calcium-channel blockers\n\nThese come from the hypertension treatment guidance (guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment), which is a major cardiology society consensus document. It says an ACE inhibitor and an ARB should not be combined, because the combination adds harm without adding benefit.\n\nLifestyle measures such as lower salt, more vegetables and fruit, regular exercise, weight reduction, less alcohol and not smoking are the shared foundation, whatever medicine is used. Whether drug treatment starts right away depends mainly on overall cardiovascular risk and the blood pressure stage:\n\n- **Stage 2** (systolic 140 or higher, or diastolic 90 or higher): drug treatment with lifestyle measures, usually starting with two agents.\n- **Stage 1 with heart disease, diabetes, kidney disease, or a 10-year cardiovascular risk of 10 percent or more:** drug treatment with lifestyle measures.\n- **Stage 1 without those features:** lifestyle measures first, then reassessment after three to six months before drugs are considered.\n\n**Where authorities differ:** The 130/80 threshold for stage 1 is a United States position. European guidance starts hypertension at 140/90 and treats 130 to 139 / 80 to 89 as high-normal. The same reading can therefore be labelled differently depending on which guidance is used. The guidance also differs on how aggressively to treat low-risk stage 1 disease, with much of the population that US guidance calls stage 1 advised toward lifestyle measures alone under European guidance.\n\n**Strength of evidence:** The retrieved material does not say how strong the evidence is for any of the four drug classes, or how they compare with one another. I could not find a passage that answers that, so I cannot tell you how confident the guidance is in these choices.\n\nThe guidance describes these as general patterns. Which option fits a particular person depends on their other health conditions and their risk, so that is a question for their clinician.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
